The Verifiability Preservation Problem: 


A Theory of Claim Verifiability, False Verification and Evidence Structure Under Archive Degradation

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

Digital preservation secures objects; science requires checkable claims. An archive may retain every byte of a result and still be unable to determine whether that result can be verified. Stodden, Krafczyk and Bhaskar (2018) obtained computational artefacts for 55 of 306 articles in the Journal of Computational Physics and found that, conditional on artefact access, findings could not easily be regenerated for 67%; no article's complete set of results could be regenerated, and the dominant barriers were inadequate documentation of code, data and workflow (70.9%) and missing licensing information (75%). Their scope is regeneration from author-supplied materials on a different computational system, not verification of scientific correctness, and not certification under controlled degradation. The complementary question remains open: when an archive is degraded, does it recognise that a claim can no longer be checked, or does it certify it away?

This dissertation develops a measurement theory for that question. A verification-cost functional κ is defined, and verifiability is shown to be monotone under removal-type degradation. A measurement triple—false verification rate, coverage, detection of unverifiable claims—is introduced, and its degenerate cases establish that no single scalar is adequate. The optimal frontier under a bound on false verification is characterised as a threshold rule on posterior verifiability, a Neyman–Pearson argument in posterior form. Certifiable mass is identified as the quantity on which architectures compete when false verification is intolerable. For unrestricted verifiers, architecture comparison reduces to comparison of information, a Blackwell refinement argument; any advantage of explicit evidence structure must therefore arise for resource-bounded verifiers. A manifest-soundness proposition locates the boundary: detection of non-verifiability does not require an evidence graph. The graph's remaining candidate justification is closure maintenance and bounded-verifier accessibility, not detection. The programme is designed so that it can answer that the graph does not earn its cost.

---

Chapter 1. The Problem

1.1 Preserved is not verifiable

A computed result is published with data, scripts, library versions, an environment description and a record of which script produced which figure. Suppose an archive loses the record of library versions. Every file may pass its checksum, the text may remain perfectly legible, and yet no independent evaluator can determine whether the claim can still be checked. A careless reader may assume that it can.

Three questions are routinely conflated:

1. Preservation: are the bits intact?
2. Reconstruction: can the content, dependencies and recorded justification be recovered?
3. Verifiability: can an independent evaluator apply the verification procedure and reach a conclusion about the evidential status of the claim?

Existing preservation practice addresses the first question well and the second partially. This dissertation concentrates on the third, and on a failure mode that has received less attention: false verification, in which an archive or its verifier declares a claim verified although the conditions for verification no longer hold. An honest verdict of "not verifiable" is a valuable output. A system that produces it more often, and correctly, may be more trustworthy than one that produces more positive results.

The empirical motivation is narrow but sufficient. Stodden et al. obtained artefacts for 55 of 306 articles in one journal with guidelines encouraging artefact availability, and among those 55 they could not easily regenerate the findings for 67%. That is a conditional failure rate under favourable access conditions, not a prevalence estimate across science. It is enough to establish that insufficient verification materials are a real and measurable condition. The present theory specifies how to measure whether an archive recognises that insufficiency honestly, rather than certifying it away.

1.2 Research questions

· RQ1 (definition). How should post-degradation verifiability be defined so that "the text survives" is not confounded with "the claim can be checked"?
· RQ2 (measurement). Which outcome measures separate honest non-verifiable verdicts from false verification, and what can be proved about their trade-off?
· RQ3 (architecture). Under what conditions can one archive architecture dominate another, and what is the role of explicit evidence structure compared with information content, dependency manifests and verifier resources?
· RQ4 (falsifiability). Which experimental design can detect a real advantage without artefacts, and what outcomes would refute the structure hypothesis?

1.3 Contributions and non-contributions

Contributions, conditional on the stated assumptions:

· C1. A formal definition of post-degradation verifiability through a verification-cost functional, with a monotonicity result.
· C2. A measurement theory built on false verification rate, coverage and detection of unverifiable claims, with a degenerate-verifier proposition and a threshold characterisation of the oracle frontier.
· C3. A comparison theory: refinement dominance, informational equivalence of structured and unstructured documentation, and the role of resource-bounded verifiers.
· C4. A boundary-locating result: detection of missing dependencies can be achieved by a manifest, so the unique value of an evidence graph, if any, lies in closure maintenance and bounded-verifier accessibility.
· C5. Design constraints for fair degradation experiments and a falsifiable programme.

Non-contributions. This work does not propose a new preservation standard or graph format, does not claim that evidence graphs improve verification, does not establish the truth of any archived claim, and does not claim that a technically recoverable archive guarantees the continuity of knowledge. Several tools used here—Neyman–Pearson thresholds, Blackwell-type comparison of experiments—are standard; the contribution is their application to this setting.

