Chapter 2. Formal Framework

2.1 Items, claims and requirements

Definition 2.1 (information items). Let 𝓘 be a universe of information items: artefacts (documents, datasets, code), relation records (typed links between items) and specifications (verification procedures, dependency declarations, environment descriptions). Each item has a persistent identifier.

Definition 2.2 (claims and requirement sets). Let C be a finite set of eligible claims, with n = |C| fixed before archive construction. For each claim c there is a preregistered verification procedure ν_c and a requirement set Req(c) ⊆ 𝓘, the items that must be accessible for ν_c to complete. Req(c) may contain other claims; its transitive closure is denoted Req*(c).

A claim is eligible only if ν_c can be applied consistently by independent evaluators. Ineligible claims—purely normative statements, claims about inaccessible instruments—are excluded from the primary analysis and reported separately.

2.2 Archive states and degradation

Definition 2.3 (state, degradation). An archive state ω has a set of surviving items A(ω) ⊆ 𝓘, held in an architecture-specific representation. A degradation scenario δ is a (possibly random) map from states to states. It is removal-type if A(δ(ω)) ⊆ A(ω). Alteration, corruption and forgery are not removal-type and are treated separately in the adversarial programme.

2.3 Verification cost and verifiability

Definition 2.4 (verification cost). For a state ω and claim c, let κ(ω, c) be the infimum of the resource cost—human time, computation, or a preregistered combination—of procedures that complete ν_c using only items in A(ω); inf ∅ = ∞.

Definition 2.5 (verifiability at budget b). θ_b(ω, c) = 1 if κ(ω, c) ≤ b, and 0 otherwise. The budget b is fixed by the protocol before the experiment.

This distinguishes two situations that the informal word "lost" conflates: absent (κ = ∞) and recoverable at a cost (finite κ above or below the budget). A removed relation record whose content can be re-derived from surviving items increases κ without making it infinite.

Assumption A1 (ignorability). Surplus surviving items never prevent a procedure from completing and never increase its cost, because a verifier may ignore items it does not need.

A1 is an idealisation, and probably false for human verifiers: surplus material is cognitive clutter that can slow, distract or mislead. The propositions below hold for idealised verifiers; the size of the violation is empirical, and the programme includes an adversarial condition that adds plausible but irrelevant material in order to test A1 directly.

Proposition 2.1 (monotonicity). Under A1, if δ is removal-type then κ(δ(ω), c) ≥ κ(ω, c) for every claim c, and hence θ_b(δ(ω), c) ≤ θ_b(ω, c) for every budget b.

Proof. Every procedure executable from A(δ(ω)) uses only items in A(ω) ⊇ A(δ(ω)), so it is executable from A(ω) at no greater cost by A1. The infimum over a larger set of procedures cannot be larger. Since θ_b is the indicator of κ ≤ b, it cannot increase. ∎

Proposition 2.1 formalises that degradation can only hurt verification, and it makes precise the rule that a claim whose verification dependency has become unavailable must not be scored as verified because its text survives.

2.4 Observations and verifiers

Definition 2.6 (observation). Under architecture X, a verifier sees an observation O_X(ω): everything it can access about the state, in the representation X provides. Two states with the same observation are indistinguishable to any verifier using X.

Definition 2.7 (verifier). A verifier is a (possibly randomised) map r from observations of a claim to a verdict in {V, U}, where V means "verified" and U means "flagged as unverifiable". A resource-bounded verifier is one whose extraction effort from the observation is limited by a budget β.

Definition 2.8 (ground truth). The label θ_b(ω, c) is assigned by a reference procedure with access to the complete pre-degradation archive and the degradation log. It is established independently of the architectures under comparison and of the verifiers being scored. The primary analysis uses controlled synthetic removal: the study builds archives in which every claim is verifiable before degradation, then removes named items and logs the removals, so that θ is known by construction from the log. Items are either removed irrecoverably (θ = 0) or left untouched. Irrecoverable removal means no duplicate copy in any replica, no external reference available to the verifier, and no cache accessible to the verifier. Cases of recoverable-at-cost loss are secondary and are labelled by a preregistered, independent re-derivation with measured reliability.

2.5 The four-cell table

For a fixed state and the fixed claim set C, each claim falls into exactly one cell:

 θ = 1 (verifiable) θ = 0 (not verifiable)
verdict V TP FP
verdict U FN TN

With TP + FP + FN + TN = n. Claims made inaccessible by degradation remain in the denominator.

