Chapter 3. Measurement Theory

3.1 The measures

False verification rate: FVR = FP / (FP + TP), the fraction of verified verdicts that are unjustified. Convention: FVR = 0 when FP + TP = 0, reported together with coverage. This convention is retained because the degenerate-verifier result depends on it.

Coverage (verification recall): ρ = TP / (TP + FN), the fraction of truly verifiable claims that are verified. When TP + FN = 0, ρ is undefined, not zero. Undefined values are reported as "n/a" with the cell counts and excluded from aggregates.

Detection of unverifiable claims: DUR = TN / (TN + FP), the fraction of truly unverifiable claims that are correctly flagged. When TN + FP = 0, DUR is undefined, not zero.

Verified reconstruction rate: R_V = TP / n, with the original denominator n.

With prevalence π = (TP + FN) / n, we have the identity R_V = ρ · π. The prevalence depends on the degradation scenario, so R_V compares architectures only under identical scenarios and identical claim sets.

Reporting rule. All proportions are reported with interval estimates. Undefined measures are reported explicitly, not silently replaced by zero. With twenty claims in the pilot, an interval around a proportion near one half is roughly twenty percentage points either side, so no effect is to be claimed from the pilot. The pilot's purpose is feasibility and measurement reliability.

Minimal sufficient reporting set. Given n and π, the four cell counts have three degrees of freedom. The pair (FVR, ρ) is therefore sufficient to reconstruct the table when π is known. DUR is retained not because it carries independent information, but because it is the quantity that most directly exposes the degenerate always-U verifier, and because reviewers of preservation systems will ask specifically what fraction of unverifiable claims were correctly flagged. The reporting rule is thus: FVR and ρ are mandatory; DUR is mandatory whenever TN + FP > 0; no composite score is reported.

3.2 Degenerate verifiers

Proposition 3.1 (degenerate verifiers).

(a) The verifier that always answers U has FVR = 0 (by convention), DUR = 1, ρ = 0 (defined here because TP + FN = n > 0) and R_V = 0.

(b) The verifier that always answers V has ρ = 1, DUR = 0 (defined here because TN + FP = n > 0) and FVR = 1 − π.

Therefore none of FVR, DUR or ρ is informative alone, and any adequate report contains at least FVR together with ρ (or R_V).

Proof. (a) With no V verdicts, FP = TP = 0 and TN = #{θ = 0}, FN = #{θ = 1}. (b) With no U verdicts, FN = TN = 0, TP = #{θ = 1}, FP = #{θ = 0}, so FVR = #{θ = 0} / n = 1 − π. ∎

This is the formal version of the warning that a system answering "impossible to verify" to everything achieves zero false verifications while being useless. The same logic forbids a single composite score at the outset: composites hide which side of the trade-off an architecture sacrificed.

3.3 The oracle frontier

Let P be the joint distribution over degradation outcomes and claims induced by the scenario family and the corpus. For an observation o, let p(o) = P(θ = 1 | O = o) be the posterior verifiability. A randomised verifier is described by q(o) ∈ [0, 1], the probability of answering V on observation o.

Proposition 3.2 (threshold characterisation of the oracle frontier). Fix α ∈ [0, 1). Among randomised verifiers using a given observation map, those maximising expected true positives subject to the constraint FVR ≤ α—in the sense FP ≤ α(FP + TP) in expectation—have the form: answer V when p(o) > t, answer U when p(o) < t, and randomise on p(o) = t, for a threshold t ≤ 1 − α determined by the constraint.

Proof. In expectation, TP = Σ_o P(o) p(o) q(o) and FP = Σ_o P(o)(1 − p(o)) q(o). The constraint FP ≤ α(FP + TP) is equivalent to Σ_o P(o)(p(o) − (1 − α)) q(o) ≥ 0. This is a linear programme in q with a linear objective and a single linear constraint. Introduce a multiplier μ ≥ 0. The Lagrangian is Σ_o P(o) q(o) [(1 + μ) p(o) − μ(1 − α)], maximised pointwise by q(o) = 1 when p(o) > μ(1 − α)/(1 + μ) and q(o) = 0 when below. Setting t = μ(1 − α)/(1 + μ) < 1 − α gives the stated form, and μ is chosen so that the constraint binds (or μ = 0 if it is slack). Strong duality holds for linear programmes. ∎

The argument is the Neyman–Pearson lemma in disguise: a likelihood-ratio threshold is replaced by a threshold on posterior verifiability. Its practical content is that a verifier should be judged against this frontier. But the frontier is oracle: p(o) is unobservable in practice. The proposition characterises the upper bound of any implementable verifier's achievable (FVR, ρ) set, with equality only if the verifier can estimate p(o) without error. It is a standard against which real verifiers are measured, not a constructive algorithm.

3.4 Certifiable mass and certification tolerance

Set α = 0, so that no false verification is tolerated.

Corollary 3.3 (certifiable mass). The maximal expected R_V at zero false verification equals m_X = P(p_X(O) = 1), the probability that the observation under architecture X certifies verifiability with certainty.

Proof. At α = 0 the constraint reads Σ_o P(o)(p(o) − 1) q(o) ≥ 0, and each term is non-positive, so q(o) = 0 wherever p(o) < 1. Verifying exactly on {p = 1} gives TP = Σ_{p(o)=1} P(o). ∎

In any realistic archive, almost no observation will certify certainty, because the requirement set is never known with certainty. This means m_X will be near zero for every architecture, and the comparison between architectures will be driven by the ambiguous mass u_X = 1 − m_X − z_X, where z_X = P(p_X(O) = 0) is certified unverifiable mass. To convert the binary idealisation into a reportable quantity, introduce a certification tolerance τ ∈ [0, 1): the maximum posterior probability of non-verifiability that the protocol treats as negligible. Define

m_X(τ) = P(p_X(O) ≥ 1 − τ).

The quantity m_X(τ) is the share of (state, claim) pairs for which the archive's observation makes non-verifiability no more likely than τ. At τ = 0 this is the strict certifiable mass; as τ increases it approaches the share of claims that are verifiable with high posterior probability. The programme reports m_X(τ) across a preregistered grid of τ values, and does not privilege any single τ.

3.5 What the theory does not provide

The posterior p(o) is unknown in practice and must be estimated, and a verifier's calibration is empirical. Ground-truth labels carry error. Propositions 3.1–3.3 therefore define what to measure and what a perfect analysis of an observation could achieve. They do not predict magnitudes.

