Chapter 4. Comparison Theory

4.1 Ambiguity makes error unavoidable

Proposition 4.1 (two-point bound). Let ω₁ and ω₂ be states with O_X(ω₁) = O_X(ω₂) for architecture X, with θ(ω₁, c) = 1 and θ(ω₂, c) = 0. For every verifier using X, the probability of a missed verification at ω₁ plus the probability of a false verification at ω₂ equals 1. Consequently the larger of the two exceeds or equals 1/2.

Proof. The verdict depends only on the observation, so P(V | ω₁) = P(V | ω₂) = v. The missed-verification probability at ω₁ is 1 − v and the false-verification probability at ω₂ is v. They sum to 1. ∎

The design question for an architecture is therefore not whether it can be perfect, but how much ambiguous mass u_X it leaves under the degradation scenarios that matter. A faithful record of what should be there is what turns an ambiguous pair into a distinguishable one.

4.2 Refinement dominance

Definition 4.1 (refinement). O_C refines O_A if there is a (possibly randomised) map g with O_A = g ∘ O_C, so that everything visible under A can be computed from what is visible under C.

Proposition 4.2 (refinement dominance). If O_C refines O_A, then:

(a) every verifier for A can be simulated by a verifier for C with the identical distribution of verdicts on every state; hence for every α the optimal coverage at FVR ≤ α under C is at least that under A, and for every pair of loss weights the Bayes risk under C is at most that under A;

(b) m_C(τ) ≥ m_A(τ) for every τ, z_C ≥ z_A and u_C ≤ u_A.

Proof. (a) Given r_A, define r_C = r_A ∘ g. On any state it produces the same verdict distribution as r_A, so every achievable error profile under A is achievable under C. (b) If p_A(o_A) = 1, then θ = 1 almost surely on the event {O_A = o_A}. That event is a union of events {O_C = o_C} with g(o_C) = o_A, and on each of them θ = 1 almost surely, so p_C = 1 there. The argument for p = 0 is symmetric. ∎

This is a special case of Blackwell's comparison of experiments, and it is deliberately elementary. Blackwell's theorem states that one information structure dominates another precisely when the latter is a garbling of the former, and the refinement condition above is exactly that garbling relation.

Corollary 4.3 (superset dominance and the added-dependency test). Suppose architecture C stores everything that B stores and only adds items; assume A1; assume degradation acts identically on the shared items (Proposition 4.5). Then O_C refines O_B and C weakly dominates B for unrestricted verifiers. A measured deficit of C relative to B beyond preregistered tolerance is therefore evidence of at least one of: C is not a true superset; surplus material harms verifiers (A1 fails); or the degradation was applied unequally.

This formalises the worry that a graph might create dependencies whose loss offsets its benefits. In this theory, a deficit cannot arise from added items alone. Where it is observed, the cause must be located among the three possibilities above.

4.3 Information versus accessibility

Proposition 4.4 (informational equivalence). If O_C refines O_D and O_D refines O_C, then for unrestricted verifiers C and D have the same optimal frontier, m_C(τ) = m_D(τ) for every τ, z_C = z_D and u_C = u_D.

Proof. Apply Proposition 4.2 in both directions. ∎

In the idealised limit, a structured evidence graph and an equally informative body of prose are equivalent. Any systematic difference between arms C and D must come from (i) differences in the information they contain, or (ii) the fact that real verifiers are bounded. Proposition 4.4 therefore makes the structure hypothesis precise and falsifiable.

4.4 Bounded verifiers: extraction cost and architecture-specific error models

Definition 4.2 (bounded verifiers and their frontier). Let the extraction effort of a verifier be a non-negative real random variable E, measured in a preregistered unit—minutes of a trained reader's time, or a fixed computational budget. For an extraction budget β ≥ 0, let 𝒱_β be the class of verifiers whose expected extraction effort satisfies E[E] ≤ β and whose extraction from the observation may err according to an extraction-error model ε.

The error model is architecture-specific. For unstructured documentation, ε_D specifies, for each observation, a probability distribution over the subset of facts the verifier recovers, indexed by β and parameterised by fact density, syntactic complexity and document length. For evidence graphs, ε_C specifies the analogous distribution, parameterised by graph size, branching factor, edge-label complexity and the proportion of implicit relations that must be inferred. Both models are preregistered and estimated in a calibration study before the pilot; neither architecture is treated as error-free by construction.

The achievable set 𝒜_β(X) ⊆ [0,1]² is the set of (FVR, ρ) pairs achievable by verifiers in 𝒱_β under architecture X. The frontier ℱ_β(X) is the upper boundary of 𝒜_β(X): the maximum coverage achievable at each FVR level, and the minimum FVR achievable at each coverage level.

Hypothesis H_S (structure hypothesis). For some realistic extraction budget β and some degradation scenarios, the frontier achievable under arm C strictly dominates that under arm D, where C and D are informationally equivalent to within a documented tolerance and construction effort is recorded.

Null H₀. Within preregistered tolerance, ℱ_β(C) = ℱ_β(D) for every tested β.

Propositions 4.2 and 4.4 give no reason to expect H_S to hold or to fail; they state exactly where an advantage must be sought. The critical methodological requirement is that ε_D and ε_C be anchored to independent evidence. For prose, the literature on reading time, fact retrieval and document complexity supplies candidate parameters. For graphs, the literature on graph comprehension and visualisation supplies candidate parameters. Without such anchors, H_S is falsifiable in principle but not in practice, because a sufficiently flexible error model can be made to favour either architecture.

4.5 Detection without structure: dependency manifests

Definition 4.3 (dependency manifest). For a claim c, a manifest is a record M_c = {(id_i, h_i) : i ∈ Req*(c)} listing the identifier and a cryptographic digest of every required item, including specifications and environment descriptions. It is created at archiving time and stored with its own integrity protection—an independent replica or a signature.

This mechanism already exists in working form. Trusty URIs embed cryptographic hash values in URIs so that a retrieved artefact can be checked to contain the content the URI is supposed to represent, and the approach has been shown to make entire reference trees verifiable, with nanopublications as the motivating application. The manifest proposition is therefore not a new mechanism; it is a boundary-location result.

Proposition 4.5 (manifest soundness). Assume: (i) the manifest was faithful at creation, that is it lists the true Req*(c); (ii) it survives intact; (iii) the digest function is collision-resistant; (iv) when all required items are present and intact, ν_c completes within budget b. Then the verifier that answers V if and only if every listed item is present with a matching digest produces no false verification under any removal-type degradation, nor under accidental alteration of required items.

Proof. If a required item is missing, the check fails; if it was altered, its digest differs except with negligible probability, and the check fails. The verdict V is issued only when all required items are present and intact, and then (iv) gives θ_b = 1. So FP = 0. ∎

The proposition is close to a tautology. Its value is boundary location: detection of non-verifiability needs no graph. Three consequences matter.

First, a BagIt-style manifest extended to requirement sets can provide detection. BagIt is an Informational RFC of October 2018, authored by Kunze, Littman, Madden, Scancella and Adams, whose payload contents are opaque to the format; per-claim requirement sets would be an extension, not a native feature.

Second, the fragile assumption is faithfulness. A manifest that omits a true dependency produces false verifications, and omission is a human or process error whose rate the programme must measure. If the omission rate is non-trivial—and the Stodden barriers of inadequate documentation and missing licensing information suggest it may be—then the manifest becomes a false-verification generator precisely because it is trusted. The programme therefore makes omission rate a primary outcome of the pilot, measured by independent re-derivation of Req*(c) by two coders blind to the manifest, with disagreement resolution by a third coder. The threshold at which B⁺ loses its zero-FVR guarantee is preregistered.

Third, a manifest that over-approximates Req*(c) will flag U when a requirement is missing yet re-derivable, producing false negatives rather than false positives. The B⁺ arm therefore trades coverage for detection in recoverable-at-cost cases, and this trade is reported separately.

4.6 Fair degradation and manipulation equivalence

Proposition 4.6 (spurious advantage of larger archives). Let I_B ⊂ I_C be the items of B and C, with N_B < N_C stored objects. A scenario that removes k objects uniformly at random from the stored set leaves each B item surviving with probability 1 − k/N_X, which is larger under C. A scenario that removes a fraction f of shared objects leaves survival probability 1 − f under both. Hence scenarios must be specified as fractions of shared objects, or must name the shared items to remove, with graph-only objects degraded by a separate, reported scenario.

Proof. Counting: under uniform removal of k objects from N, each object survives with probability (N − k)/N. ∎

Manipulation equivalence. Removing a contradiction link from a graph has no obvious twin in prose. The prose arms must lose the information the link carries—for example, the sentence stating the contradiction. Before the experiment, for each structured element to be removed, the protocol requires: (i) a written statement of the information the element carries; (ii) the corresponding prose sentence or table entry in Arm D that carries the same information; (iii) confirmation by two independent reviewers, blind to arm, that the removal is informationally equivalent across arms; (iv) a resolution rule for disagreement: a third reviewer and, if still unresolved, exclusion of the affected claim from the primary analysis and reporting as a residual asymmetry. Failure to apply this protocol would make every arm comparison an artefact of the manipulation.

