Chapter 5. Predictions and Falsification

The theory generates qualitative predictions. None asserts a magnitude, and each states what would count against it.

P1 (irrecoverable removal). A required item is removed and cannot be recovered. Prediction: θ = 0 in every arm; coverage is identical; arms differ only in FVR and DUR. A result in which an arm verifies such claims indicates a ground-truth error or leakage from an outside copy.

P2 (requirement record erased). The record of requirements is erased. Prediction: ambiguity rises in every arm; false verification is expected where no surviving record of expectation exists. A result in which an arm with an intact, faithful manifest shows clearly higher FVR than an arm without one would count against the manifest-soundness proposition.

P3 (relations lost, content intact). Prediction: κ rises but stays finite; coverage differences arise only for bounded verifiers. A result in which C equals D at every tested β supports H₀, not H_S.

P4 (superset). C stores everything B stores plus a graph. Prediction: C weakly dominates B for unrestricted verifiers. A result in which C is worse than B beyond tolerance requires locating the cause among the three possibilities of Corollary 4.3.

P5a (environment specification). The execution environment is unavailable. Prediction: no arm recreates the environment; arms differ in whether they record what would be needed. A result in which an arm records the specification without access to any hidden copy would indicate a design failure.

P5b (environment recreation). Prediction: no arm restores the environment; arms differ only in detection. A result in which an arm restores it without access to the environment indicates a hidden copy.

P6 (construction effort). Prediction: cost ordering K_C ≥ K_{B⁺} ≥ K_B is plausible. A reversed ordering indicates a measurement problem or a hidden cost transfer.

Adoption rule. Prefer arm C over B⁺ only if the measured reduction in expected loss, with preregistered loss weights λ_FP for false verification and λ_FN for missed verification, exceeds the measured difference in construction and maintenance cost. The comparison uses interval estimates, not point estimates, and the decision threshold is preregistered. The weights are a decision input and must be justified before the experiment, not tuned afterwards. The Bayes-optimal verdict at loss weights (λ_FP, λ_FN) is V exactly when p(o) ≥ λ_FP / (λ_FP + λ_FN); as false verification becomes intolerable the threshold approaches 1 and coverage depends entirely on certifiable mass.

A1 test. The surplus-material condition is preregistered as a factorial manipulation: surplus present/absent × architecture. The prediction is that the interaction term is the quantity of interest. If C with graph suffers less from adversarial surplus than D with prose, that is evidence for structure under bounded verification even when FVR is equal. If surplus harms both equally, A1 is approximately true and the idealised propositions apply.

