Chapter 6. Research Programme and Status

6.1 Phase 0: audit and Gate G0

Fill the requirements grid from primary specifications with exact section references; perform the literature audit; test Hypothesis G. Gate G0: if equivalent evaluations already exist, narrow or abandon the contribution; if existing standards cover every needed capability, build on them and recentre the work on the evaluation protocol.

The first-pass audit has verified the following. Stodden, Krafczyk and Bhaskar (2018) matches on the 55-of-306 denominator, the 67% conditional failure rate, and the barrier percentages of 70.9% and 75%; its scope is regeneration from author-supplied materials on a different computational system, not correctness and not controlled degradation. The four-hour cap belongs to the 2021 follow-up by Krafczyk, Shi, Bhaskar, Marinov and Stodden, and is cited separately. BagIt is confirmed as an Informational RFC of October 2018 with the author list given above. Micropublications is confirmed as a semantic model of claims, evidence, arguments and annotations, whose maximal form is a statement with its complete supporting argument. Trusty URIs are confirmed as the existing mechanism for hash-based verification of reference trees. Blackwell (1951) is confirmed as the source of the garbling/refinement comparison of experiments. Neyman–Pearson (1933) is confirmed as the threshold lemma.

The remaining audit item is the section-level reading of RO-Crate and OCFL. OCFL specifies completeness in the sense that a repository can be rebuilt from the files it stores, and fixity as a by-product of content-based addressing; it does not, in the sections seen, define a claim-level verification predicate. RO-Crate specifies a method of aggregating and describing research data with metadata; the normative sections seen define contextual entities and JSON-LD structure, not a verification predicate θ_b at claim level. If either specification is found to define claim-level verification, Hypothesis G is refuted and the contribution narrows further. The present status of Gate G0 is therefore: audit pending, no equivalent verification predicate found in limited search.

6.2 Phase 1: feasibility and calibration

Encode two or three computable claims in existing formats—micropublications, nanopublications, RO-Crate with PROV—and simulate loss of relations while keeping content. Record what each format cannot express.

Run a calibration study before the pilot that estimates fact-recovery rates from prose and from graph representations under time pressure, and use those estimates to fix ε_D and ε_C. If the calibration shows that prose and graph recovery rates are indistinguishable at realistic budgets, H_S is dead and the programme reports that honestly. If it shows a gap, the programme has a parameter, not a hope.

6.3 Phase 2: measurement protocol

Define the verification procedures ν_c, the budget b, the ground-truth procedure (controlled synthetic removal with a removal log, Definition 2.8) and its reliability, scoring of TP, FP, FN and TN, and reporting of FVR, ρ, DUR and R_V without a composite score. Separate the people who build archives from those who evaluate them where possible; blind evaluators to arm; audit manifest faithfulness and report omission rate as a primary outcome; estimate inter-rater agreement. Apply the manipulation-equivalence protocol of §4.6. Report undefined measures explicitly. Include a power analysis stating the minimum detectable effect given the planned confirmatory sample size, and commit to a sequential or adaptive design if the pilot suggests a smaller effect than the design can detect.

6.4 Phase 3: pilot

About twenty computable claims from public projects with code, data and automatable tests; arms A (version-controlled repository), B (OAIS-informed), B⁺ (B plus manifests, no graph), C (evidence-structured) and D (informationally equivalent unstructured documentation); at least three scenarios covering removal of a dependency, loss of provenance metadata and unavailability of the environment, specified as fractions or shared-object removals. The pilot's purpose is feasibility, measurement reliability, calibration of ε_D and ε_C, and estimation of manifest omission rate. It is not designed to detect a structure effect.

Arm D serialisation protocol. Take the fact set encoded in Arm C. Serialise it as prose and tables without using any structured schema. Two independent coders, blind to arm and to the other coder's output, receive the fact set of Arm C, the prose and tables of Arm D, and the schema used in Arm C, with unlimited time and full schema access. They judge whether every fact in C is recoverable from D and every fact in D is recoverable from C, to a preregistered tolerance. Disagreements go to a third coder. Only after this information-level equivalence is established is the bounded-verifier comparison run. This ensures that the C-versus-D comparison tests accessibility, not missing information.

Primary outcomes by comparison. For A versus B, FVR is the natural outcome. For B⁺ versus C, coverage at zero FVR—or at a preregistered certification tolerance τ—is the natural outcome. Mixing them into a single outcome obscures the comparison. The programme therefore preregisters separate primary outcomes for each planned contrast.

6.5 Phase 4: preregistered confirmatory study

Freeze corpus, arms, scenarios, primary outcomes, thresholds, sample size and analysis, based on the pilot and the calibration study.

6.6 Phase 5: human intelligibility

A separate preregistered reader study on archived material under degradation, with independent readers and ethics review. The present theory does not cover intelligibility, and technical recoverability must not be assumed to imply it.

6.7 Phase 6: release

Publish protocol, scripts, results including negative results, and limitations. Keep versioned copies independent of the primary hosting platform, with published checksums and restoration instructions.

