Chapter 7. Limitations and Conclusion

7.1 Limitations

The theory is conditional on A1, on reliable ground truth and on a faithful requirement specification. Each is an empirical assumption. The cost functional κ depends on a preregistered notion of resource and on a human or computational verifier; defining it consistently is difficult. Definition 4.2 makes the bounded-verifier frontier explicit for both architectures, but ε_D and ε_C must be estimated in the calibration study and may prove unreliable. Some claims cannot be verified from an archive at all—physical experiments, inaccessible instruments—and are excluded from the primary analysis, which limits generality. Intelligibility is audience-dependent and not covered by the formal theory. Adversarial degradation—forged provenance, compromised credentials, coordinated replica corruption—requires a separate security model; Proposition 4.5 assumes accidental degradation and collision-resistant digests, and does not protect against a compromised manifest. Nothing here shows that any archive survives indefinitely or that a recoverable archive guarantees the continuity of knowledge.

