7.3 Conclusion

The original aspiration was an archive that lets future generations recover, assess and correct knowledge. This dissertation reduces that aspiration to a question that can fail: after degradation, how often does an archive say a claim is verifiable when it is not, how often does it correctly say it is not, and what does the structure that supports honest answers cost?

Stodden, Krafczyk and Bhaskar (2018) document a high conditional failure rate in one journal and one artefact-access condition: among the 55 of 306 articles for which artefacts were obtained, findings could not easily be regenerated for 67%, and no article's complete set of results could be regenerated. That is not a prevalence estimate, but it establishes that insufficient verification materials are real and measurable. The theory specifies how to measure whether an archive recognises that insufficiency honestly, rather than certifying it away.

The contribution is deliberately narrowed. It is not a new preservation standard and not a new graph format. The manifest result locates the boundary: a BagIt-style manifest extended to requirement sets, or a Trusty-URI-style digest tree, can detect non-verifiability without any evidence graph. The graph's remaining candidate justification is closure maintenance—the consistent revision of Req*(c) under contradiction and supersession—and bounded-verifier accessibility. Whether explicit structure earns its cost on those dimensions is an empirical matter. The programme is designed so that it can answer no.


Appendices

Appendix A. Formal Proofs and Derivations

This appendix records the proofs of all propositions and corollaries stated in the main text, together with the auxiliary lemmas on which they depend. The proofs are elementary by design; their function is to make the logical dependencies between definitions and conclusions inspectable, not to display technical difficulty.

A.1 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Monotonicity)

Proposition 2.1. Under A1, if δ is removal-type then κ(δ(ω), c) ≥ κ(ω, c) for every claim c, and hence θ_b(δ(ω), c) ≤ θ_b(ω, c) for every budget b.

Proof. Let δ be removal-type, so A(δ(ω)) ⊆ A(ω). Let Π(ω) be the set of procedures that complete ν_c using only items in A(ω), and Π(δ(ω)) the corresponding set for A(δ(ω)). Since A(δ(ω)) ⊆ A(ω), every procedure in Π(δ(ω)) is also in Π(ω): a procedure that uses only items available in the degraded state uses only items available in the original state. Hence Π(δ(ω)) ⊆ Π(ω).

By Definition 2.4, κ(ω, c) = inf_{π ∈ Π(ω)} cost(π) and κ(δ(ω), c) = inf_{π ∈ Π(δ(ω))} cost(π). The infimum over a subset cannot be smaller than the infimum over the superset: if Π(δ(ω)) ⊆ Π(ω), then inf Π(ω) ≤ inf Π(δ(ω)). Therefore κ(δ(ω), c) ≥ κ(ω, c).

For the second claim, θ_b(ω, c) = 1 if and only if κ(ω, c) ≤ b. Since κ(δ(ω), c) ≥ κ(ω, c), the condition κ(δ(ω), c) ≤ b implies κ(ω, c) ≤ b. Hence θ_b(δ(ω), c) ≤ θ_b(ω, c). ∎

Remark A.1. The proof uses A1 only in the identification of Π(δ(ω)) as a subset of Π(ω) at no greater cost. Without A1, a procedure in Π(δ(ω)) might incur a different cost when executed on A(ω), because surplus items could interfere. The set inclusion Π(δ(ω)) ⊆ Π(ω) remains true as sets of procedures, but the cost functional need not be monotone in the item set. This is the precise point at which A1 enters, and it is the point that the adversarial surplus condition is designed to test.

A.2 Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Degenerate Verifiers)

Proposition 3.1. (a) The verifier that always answers U has FVR = 0 (by convention), DUR = 1, ρ = 0 (defined here because TP + FN = n > 0) and R_V = 0. (b) The verifier that always answers V has ρ = 1, DUR = 0 (defined here because TN + FP = n > 0) and FVR = 1 − π.

Proof. (a) Always-U: no verdict is V, so TP = FP = 0. The U verdicts are issued on all n claims, so FN = #{θ = 1} and TN = #{θ = 0}. Then FVR = FP/(FP + TP) = 0/0, set to 0 by convention. DUR = TN/(TN + FP) = TN/TN = 1 because TN + FP = #{θ = 0} + 0 = n − #{θ = 1}. If #{θ = 1} = n, then TN = 0 and DUR is undefined by the reporting rule; but the proposition is stated for the generic case in which both θ-classes are non-empty. ρ = TP/(TP + FN) = 0/(0 + FN) = 0, defined because TP + FN = #{θ = 1} = n − #{θ = 0} > 0 in the generic case. R_V = TP/n = 0.

(b) Always-V: no verdict is U, so FN = TN = 0. TP = #{θ = 1} and FP = #{θ = 0}. Then ρ = TP/(TP + FN) = TP/TP = 1, defined because TP + FN = #{θ = 1} > 0. DUR = TN/(TN + FP) = 0/FP = 0, defined because TN + FP = #{θ = 0} > 0. FVR = FP/(FP + TP) = #{θ = 0}/n = 1 − #{θ = 1}/n = 1 − π. ∎

Corollary A.1 (No single scalar suffices). For any scalar functional f(TP, FP, FN, TN), there exist two verifiers with identical f-value but different (FVR, ρ) profiles.

Proof. The always-U verifier has (FVR, ρ) = (0, 0); the always-V verifier has (FVR, ρ) = (1 − π, 1). Any scalar f that assigns the same value to both must be constant on these two profiles. But a third verifier—for example, one that answers V exactly on the θ = 1 claims, achieving (FVR, ρ) = (0, 1)—has a different profile. If f is not constant, it must distinguish at least one pair; if it is constant, it is uninformative. In either case f cannot order all verifiers consistently with the (FVR, ρ) partial order. ∎

A.3 Proof of Proposition 3.2 (Threshold Characterisation of the Oracle Frontier)

Proposition 3.2. Fix α ∈ [0, 1). Among randomised verifiers using a given observation map, those maximising expected true positives subject to the constraint FVR ≤ α—in the sense FP ≤ α(FP + TP) in expectation—have the form: answer V when p(o) > t, answer U when p(o) < t, and randomise on p(o) = t, for a threshold t ≤ 1 − α determined by the constraint.

Proof. Let q: 𝒪 → [0, 1] be the probability of answering V on observation o. In expectation over the joint distribution of (O, θ),

TP(q) = Σ_{o ∈ 𝒪} P(o) p(o) q(o),  FP(q) = Σ_{o ∈ 𝒪} P(o) (1 − p(o)) q(o).

The constraint FP ≤ α(FP + TP) is equivalent to

FP − α(FP + TP) ≤ 0
⇔ Σ_o P(o) [(1 − p(o)) − α((1 − p(o)) + p(o))] q(o) ≤ 0
⇔ Σ_o P(o) [(1 − p(o)) − α] q(o) ≤ 0
⇔ Σ_o P(o) [p(o) − (1 − α)] q(o) ≥ 0.

The last line reverses the inequality because −[(1 − p) − α] = p − (1 − α). The optimisation problem is therefore:

maximise  Σ_o P(o) p(o) q(o)
subject to  Σ_o P(o) [p(o) − (1 − α)] q(o) ≥ 0,
0 ≤ q(o) ≤ 1 for all o.

This is a linear programme in q with a linear objective and a single linear constraint. Introduce a multiplier μ ≥ 0 for the constraint. The Lagrangian is

ℒ(q, μ) = Σ_o P(o) q(o) [p(o) + μ(p(o) − (1 − α))]
= Σ_o P(o) q(o) [(1 + μ) p(o) − μ(1 − α)].

For fixed μ, ℒ is maximised pointwise by setting q(o) = 1 when the bracket is positive, q(o) = 0 when it is negative, and any value in [0, 1] when it is zero. The bracket is positive exactly when

(1 + μ) p(o) > μ(1 − α)
⇔ p(o) > μ(1 − α)/(1 + μ).

Set t(μ) = μ(1 − α)/(1 + μ). Since μ ≥ 0 and α < 1, t(μ) < 1 − α. The multiplier μ is chosen so that the constraint binds, or μ = 0 if it is slack. Strong duality holds for linear programmes, so the pointwise maximiser of the Lagrangian at the optimal μ is an optimal solution of the primal problem. This gives the stated threshold form: answer V when p(o) > t, answer U when p(o) < t, randomise on p(o) = t. ∎

Remark A.2 (Oracle status). The threshold t depends on p(o), which is a posterior probability conditional on the observation. In any implementable verifier, p(o) must be estimated, and estimation error means the implementable frontier lies weakly below the oracle frontier. Proposition 3.2 characterises the upper bound, not a constructive algorithm.

A.4 Proof of Corollary 3.3 (Certifiable Mass)

Corollary 3.3. The maximal expected R_V at zero false verification equals m_X = P(p_X(O) = 1), the probability that the observation under architecture X certifies verifiability with certainty.

Proof. Set α = 0 in the constraint of Proposition 3.2:

Σ_o P(o) [p(o) − 1] q(o) ≥ 0.

Since p(o) ≤ 1 for every o, each term p(o) − 1 is non-positive. For the sum to be non-negative, every term with q(o) > 0 must have p(o) = 1 (otherwise a negative term contributes). Hence q(o) = 0 wherever p(o) < 1, and q(o) may be 1 wherever p(o) = 1. The maximal expected true-positive mass is therefore

TP = Σ_{o: p(o)=1} P(o) · 1 = P(p(O) = 1).

Since R_V = TP/n and the expectation is taken over the (state, claim) distribution, the maximal expected R_V is P(p_X(O) = 1) = m_X. ∎

Corollary A.2 (Certification tolerance). For τ ∈ [0, 1), define m_X(τ) = P(p_X(O) ≥ 1 − τ). Then m_X(τ) is non-decreasing in τ, m_X(0) = m_X, and m_X(τ) → P(p_X(O) > 0) as τ → 1.

Proof. The events {p ≥ 1 − τ} are nested: if τ₁ ≤ τ₂, then 1 − τ₁ ≥ 1 − τ₂, so {p ≥ 1 − τ₁} ⊆ {p ≥ 1 − τ₂}. Probability is monotone under inclusion, giving m_X(τ₁) ≤ m_X(τ₂). At τ = 0 the event is {p ≥ 1}, which is {p = 1} because p ≤ 1, so m_X(0) = m_X. As τ → 1, the event increases to {p > 0} (since p ≥ 1 − τ for some τ < 1 iff p > 0), and by continuity from below m_X(τ) → P(p > 0). ∎

A.5 Proof of Proposition 4.1 (Two-Point Bound)

Proposition 4.1. Let ω₁ and ω₂ be states with O_X(ω₁) = O_X(ω₂) for architecture X, with θ(ω₁, c) = 1 and θ(ω₂, c) = 0. For every verifier using X, the probability of a missed verification at ω₁ plus the probability of a false verification at ω₂ equals 1. Consequently the larger of the two exceeds or equals 1/2.

Proof. Since O_X(ω₁) = O_X(ω₂) = o, and the verifier r is a function of the observation (possibly randomised), the distribution of the verdict is the same at both states: P(V | ω₁) = P(V | ω₂) = v for some v ∈ [0, 1]. At ω₁, θ = 1, so a missed verification occurs when the verdict is U, with probability 1 − v. At ω₂, θ = 0, so a false verification occurs when the verdict is V, with probability v. Their sum is (1 − v) + v = 1. Since the two probabilities are non-negative and sum to 1, max(1 − v, v) ≥ 1/2. ∎

Corollary A.3 (Ambiguity mass lower bound). If a set of (state, claim) pairs has ambiguous mass u_X > 0, then for every verifier using X, the sum of the expected missed-verification rate and the expected false-verification rate on that set is at least u_X/2.

Proof. Partition the ambiguous set into pairs (ω₁, ω₂) with the same observation and opposite θ, as in Proposition 4.1. On each pair, the sum of the two error probabilities is 1. Integrating over the ambiguous mass gives a total error mass of at least u_X · (1/2) on one side or the other, hence the sum of the two expected rates is at least u_X. A sharper bound of u_X/2 follows by applying the two-point bound to the worse side. ∎

A.6 Proof of Proposition 4.2 (Refinement Dominance)

Proposition 4.2. If O_C refines O_A, then: (a) every verifier for A can be simulated by a verifier for C with the identical distribution of verdicts on every state; hence for every α the optimal coverage at FVR ≤ α under C is at least that under A, and for every pair of loss weights the Bayes risk under C is at most that under A; (b) m_C(τ) ≥ m_A(τ) for every τ, z_C ≥ z_A and u_C ≤ u_A.

Proof. (a) By Definition 4.1, there is a map g with O_A = g ∘ O_C. Given a verifier r_A for A, define r_C = r_A ∘ g. For any state ω, r_C(O_C(ω)) = r_A(g(O_C(ω))) = r_A(O_A(ω)), so r_C produces exactly the same verdict as r_A on every state. Hence every verdict distribution achievable under A is achievable under C. The set of achievable (FVR, ρ) profiles under A is therefore a subset of that under C, so the upper boundary ℱ_β(C) lies weakly above ℱ_β(A) for every β, and the Bayes risk under C is at most that under A for every loss weighting.

(b) Suppose p_A(o_A) = 1 for some observation o_A under A. The event {O_A = o_A} is the union of events {O_C = o_C} over all o_C with g(o_C) = o_A. On each such event, θ = 1 almost surely, because conditional on O_A = o_A the probability of θ = 1 is 1 and the finer observation O_C cannot increase the probability of θ = 0. Hence p_C(o_C) = 1 for each o_C in the preimage. Therefore P(p_C(O_C) = 1) ≥ P(p_A(O_A) = 1), i.e. m_C ≥ m_A. The same argument with θ = 0 gives z_C ≥ z_A. Since u_X = 1 − m_X − z_X and m_C ≥ m_A, z_C ≥ z_A, we have u_C ≤ u_A. The argument for m_X(τ) is identical with the event {p ≥ 1 − τ} in place of {p = 1}. ∎

Remark A.3 (Blackwell). The refinement condition O_A = g ∘ O_C is exactly the statement that experiment A is a garbling of experiment C in the sense of Blackwell (1951). Blackwell’s theorem states that C is more informative than A—in the sense that every expected-utility maximiser weakly prefers C—if and only if A is a garbling of C. Proposition 4.2(a) is the decision-theoretic half of that equivalence, specialised to the binary verdict problem.

A.7 Proof of Proposition 4.4 (Informational Equivalence)

Proposition 4.4. If O_C refines O_D and O_D refines O_C, then for unrestricted verifiers C and D have the same optimal frontier, m_C(τ) = m_D(τ) for every τ, z_C = z_D and u_C = u_D.

Proof. By Proposition 4.2, O_C refines O_D implies ℱ_β(C) ≥ ℱ_β(D) and m_C ≥ m_D, z_C ≥ z_D, u_C ≤ u_D. The reverse refinement gives the reverse inequalities. Hence equality holds in all cases. ∎

A.8 Proof of Proposition 4.5 (Manifest Soundness)

Proposition 4.5. Assume: (i) the manifest was faithful at creation, that is it lists the true Req(c); (ii) it survives intact; (iii) the digest function is collision-resistant; (iv) when all required items are present and intact, ν_c completes within budget b. Then the verifier that answers V if and only if every listed item is present with a matching digest produces no false verification under any removal-type degradation, nor under accidental alteration of required items.*

Proof. Let r_M be the manifest verifier: r_M(ω, c) = V iff for every (id_i, h_i) ∈ M_c, the item with identifier id_i is present in A(ω) and its digest equals h_i.

Suppose r_M(ω, c) = V. Then every listed item is present and unaltered. By faithfulness (i), the listed items are exactly Req*(c). By (iv), ν_c completes within budget b on these items, so θ_b(ω, c) = 1. Hence FP = 0: every V verdict is a true positive.

Conversely, suppose some required item is removed (removal-type degradation). Then the manifest lists an identifier that is absent, so the presence check fails and r_M returns U, not V. If a required item is altered accidentally, its digest differs from h_i except with negligible probability by collision resistance (iii), so the digest check fails and r_M returns U. In neither case does r_M issue a V verdict on a claim with θ_b = 0. ∎

Corollary A.4 (Omission causes false verification). If the manifest omits a true dependency d ∈ Req(c) \ M_c, and d is removed by degradation while all listed items survive, then r_M returns V although θ_b = 0.*

Proof. Since d is omitted from M_c, the manifest check does not test for its presence. If d is removed but all listed items survive with matching digests, r_M returns V. But d ∈ Req*(c) is required for ν_c, so κ(ω, c) = ∞ and θ_b(ω, c) = 0. Hence r_M produces a false verification. ∎

Remark A.4 (Omission rate as a primary quantity). Corollary A.4 identifies the exact mechanism by which a trusted manifest becomes a false-verification generator. If the omission rate per claim is ε_omit, then the manifest verifier’s FVR is bounded below by the probability that an omitted dependency is removed while all listed dependencies survive. Under independent removal of a fraction f, this is approximately ε_omit · f · (1 − f)^{|M_c|}, which is increasing in ε_omit and f. The pilot measures ε_omit directly by independent re-derivation of Req*(c).

A.9 Proof of Proposition 4.6 (Spurious Advantage of Larger Archives)

Proposition 4.6. Let I_B ⊂ I_C be the items of B and C, with N_B < N_C stored objects. A scenario that removes k objects uniformly at random from the stored set leaves each B item surviving with probability 1 − k/N_X, which is larger under C. A scenario that removes a fraction f of shared objects leaves survival probability 1 − f under both.

Proof. Uniform removal of k objects from a set of N: the number of objects surviving is N − k, and by symmetry each object survives with probability (N − k)/N = 1 − k/N. Since N_B < N_C, 1 − k/N_B < 1 − k/N_C, so each shared B item has a higher survival probability under C. This is the spurious advantage: C appears more robust only because it stores more objects, not because its architecture is better.

If instead a fraction f of the shared objects is removed, and the shared set has size N_shared, then k = f · N_shared objects are removed from the shared set in both arms. Each shared object survives with probability 1 − f in both arms, because the removal is defined on the shared set, not on the total stored set. Graph-only objects in C are degraded by a separate, reported scenario. ∎

A.10 Auxiliary Lemma: The Four-Cell Table Has Three Degrees of Freedom

Lemma A.1. Given n and the prevalence π = (TP + FN)/n, the four cell counts (TP, FP, FN, TN) are determined by any two of the measures FVR, ρ, DUR together with n and π.

Proof. The constraints are TP + FP + FN + TN = n and TP + FN = πn. These are two linear equations in four unknowns, leaving two degrees of freedom. Specifying FVR = FP/(FP + TP) and ρ = TP/(TP + FN) gives two additional equations, so the system is determined. Specifying FVR and DUR similarly determines the table: DUR = TN/(TN + FP) gives TN in terms of FP, and the total constraint then determines FN. ∎

Remark A.5. Lemma A.1 is the formal basis for the claim in §3.1 that (FVR, ρ) is a minimal sufficient reporting pair when π is known. DUR is retained for interpretive reasons—it is the measure that most directly exposes the always-U verifier—not because it carries independent information.

