Appendix B. Source Verification Register

This appendix records the primary-source checks performed for the final draft. Each entry states what was verified, against which source, and what remains unresolved.

B.1 Stodden, Krafczyk and Bhaskar (2018)

Verified against the ACM abstract and the Stanford author PDF. The abstract states: “We find that, for the articles for which we obtained computational artifacts, we could not easily regenerate the findings for 67% of them, and we were unable to easily regenerate all the findings for any of the articles. We then evaluated the artifacts we did obtain (55 of 306 articles) and find that the main barriers to computational reproducibility are inadequate documentation of code, data, and workflow information (70.9%), missing code function and setting information, and missing licensing information (75%).”

The scope statement is also verified: “we take a narrow interpretation of this goal, and attempt to regenerate published claims from author-supplied information, including data, code, inputs, and other provided specifications, on a different computational system than that used by the original authors.” This confirms that the 67% figure is conditional on artefact access, is not a prevalence estimate, and does not measure correctness or false certification under controlled degradation.

B.2 Krafczyk, Shi, Bhaskar, Marinov and Stodden (2021)

Verified as a separate publication. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A 379(2197): 20200069, published 29 March 2021. The paper reports reproduction efforts for seven published articles and introduces three principles and the Reproduction Package. The four-hour protocol is associated with this follow-up, not with the 2018 workshop paper. In the final register the two sources are cited separately, and the four-hour cap is attributed to the 2021 paper.

B.3 RFC 8493 (BagIt)

Verified against the RFC Editor rendering. Category: Informational. October 2018. Authors: J. Kunze (California Digital Library), J. Littman (Stanford Libraries), E. Madden (Library of Congress), J. Scancella (Library of Congress), C. Adams (Library of Congress). The abstract states: “A ‘bag’ has just enough structure to enclose descriptive metadata ‘tags’ and a file ‘payload’ but does not require knowledge of the payload’s internal semantics.” This confirms that per-claim requirement sets would be an extension, not a native feature.

B.4 Micropublications (Clark, Ciccarese and Goble, 2014)

Verified against the PubMed/PMC record and the additional material. Journal of Biomedical Semantics 5(1): 28, DOI 10.1186/2041-1480-5-28. The model supports claims, evidence, arguments, annotations, challenge and support relations, similarity groups, and layering; it is grounded in argumentation theory. This is the closest conceptual prior art to the evidence graph, and the final draft cites it as such.

B.5 Trusty URIs (Kuhn and Dumontier, 2014)

Verified against the ESWC 2014 proceedings record. The technique embeds cryptographic hash values in URIs so that “not only specific resources but their entire reference trees [are] verifiable.” Digital artifacts can be identified at more abstract levels than the byte level, including RDF graphs, so resources keep their hash values across serialisation formats. This is the existing mechanism that Proposition 4.5 formalises; the proposition is a boundary-location result, not a new mechanism.

B.6 Blackwell (1951) and Neyman–Pearson (1933)

Blackwell (1951). The comparison of experiments gives a partial order: experiment I is more Blackwell-informative than experiment II if the latter is a garbling of the former. The refinement condition of Definition 4.1 is exactly the garbling relation, and Proposition 4.2(a) is the decision-theoretic half of Blackwell’s equivalence.

Neyman–Pearson (1933). Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A 231(694–706): 289–337, published 16 February 1933. The lemma is the threshold characterisation of most powerful tests; Proposition 3.2 is its posterior form, with a threshold on p(o) in place of a likelihood ratio.

B.7 OCFL and RO-Crate: unresolved audit items

OCFL. The specification describes completeness (“a repository can be rebuilt purely from the files it stores”) and fixity as built into content-addressing; validation uses inventory.json. Fixity blocks are validated for file existence, but the OCFL does not validate digests for all possible algorithms. In the sections seen, OCFL defines object-level completeness and fixity, not a claim-level verification predicate θ_b.

RO-Crate. The specification defines a method of aggregating and describing data with metadata, including provenance for files (how they were created, with what software and equipment). A workflow-run RO-Crate profile is planned to use Schema.org provenance for input/output boundaries. In the sections seen, RO-Crate captures provenance and aggregation, not a claim-level verification predicate.

Status of Gate G0. The limited search found adjacent work on archive metrics, computational reproducibility and execution preservation, but no source that measures false certification under controlled degradation. The OCFL and RO-Crate audits are recorded as pending at section level; if either defines a claim-level verification predicate, Hypothesis G is refuted and the contribution narrows further. At present the register records: no equivalent verification predicate found in limited search.

