Appendix C. Measurement Protocol: Operational Details

C.1 Ground-truth construction by controlled synthetic removal

The primary analysis builds archives in which every claim in C is verifiable before degradation. For each claim c, the requirement set Req(c) is enumerated and each item is assigned a persistent identifier and a digest. Degradation scenarios remove named items irrecoverably: no duplicate copy in any replica, no external reference available to the verifier, and no cache accessible to the verifier. The removal log records, for each claim and each scenario, which required items were removed. The ground-truth label is then θ_b(ω, c) = 0 if any item in Req(c) was removed and ν_c cannot complete within b using the surviving items; θ_b(ω, c) = 1 otherwise.

Recoverable-at-cost cases (finite κ above b) are handled in a secondary analysis: the label is assigned by an independent re-derivation procedure with measured reliability, and the reporting distinguishes “absent” (κ = ∞) from “recoverable above budget” (b < κ < ∞).

C.2 Cell scoring and interval estimation

For each arm and scenario, the four cell counts are recorded directly. Proportions are reported with Wilson score intervals at the claim level, with a cluster-robust correction if claims are nested within projects. Undefined measures (ρ when TP + FN = 0; DUR when TN + FP = 0) are reported as “n/a” with the cell counts, never as zero. The FVR convention (FVR = 0 when FP + TP = 0) is reported together with coverage, and the convention is flagged explicitly in every table where it is used.

C.3 Manifest omission rate

Omission rate is measured by independent re-derivation of Req*(c) by two coders blind to the manifest, with disagreement resolved by a third coder. For each claim, the omission indicator is 1 if the manifest omits any item that the re-derivation includes. The omission rate is the mean indicator over claims, reported with an interval. The threshold at which B⁺ loses its zero-FVR guarantee is preregistered as a function of the omission rate and the removal fraction.

C.4 Calibration of extraction-error models

Before the pilot, a calibration study estimates fact-recovery rates from prose and from graph representations under time pressure. The unit of extraction effort is minutes of trained reader time. The fact set is the union of facts encoded in Arm C; the prose serialisation in Arm D is constructed by the serialisation protocol of §6.4. Recovery is scored as the fraction of facts correctly retrieved within the budget. The estimated recovery rates fix the parameters of ε_D and ε_C. If the calibration shows no distinguishable recovery rates at realistic budgets, H_S is reported as not supported and the bounded-verifier comparison is not asserted.

C.5 Power analysis and sequential design

The confirmatory study preregisters a minimum detectable effect for the primary contrast (coverage at fixed FVR for B⁺ vs C). If the pilot suggests an effect smaller than the design can detect, the study adopts a sequential or adaptive design with a preregistered stopping rule. The pilot's twenty claims are not used to estimate the confirmatory effect size; they are used to estimate measurement reliability and the variance of the cell counts.

---

Appendix D. Degradation Scenarios and Manipulation Equivalence

D.1 Scenario specification

Scenarios are specified as fractions of shared objects, or as named removals of shared items, so that larger archives do not receive a spurious survival advantage (Proposition 4.6). The minimum scenario set is:

· S1 (dependency removal): removal of a specified fraction of shared dependency items, with graph-only items degraded separately.
· S2 (provenance metadata loss): removal of provenance records that link scripts to outputs, with the corresponding prose sentences removed in Arm D.
· S3 (environment unavailability): removal of environment specifications and version records, with the corresponding prose descriptions removed in Arm D.

Each scenario is applied independently and in combination. The removal log records every removal at the item level.

D.2 Manipulation equivalence protocol

For each structured element e to be removed in Arm C, the protocol preregisters: (i) the information content of e, stated in a fixed format; (ii) the corresponding prose sentence or table entry in Arm D that carries the same information; (iii) confirmation by two independent reviewers, blind to arm, that removing e from C and removing its counterpart from D are informationally equivalent; (iv) a resolution rule: a third reviewer, and if still unresolved, exclusion of the affected claim from the primary analysis with the residual asymmetry reported. The disagreement rate is reported as a measure of protocol reliability.

D.3 Adversarial surplus condition

The surplus condition is a factorial manipulation: surplus present/absent × architecture. Surplus material includes old versions of manifests, plausible but incorrect dependencies, and redundant provenance records. The prediction is that the interaction term is the quantity of interest: if C with graph suffers less from adversarial surplus than D with prose, that is evidence for structure under bounded verification even when FVR is equal. If surplus harms both equally, A1 is approximately true and the idealised propositions apply.

