G.15 The A1 Surplus Condition as a Factorial Design

```
                         SURPLUS ABSENT        SURPLUS PRESENT
                      +---------------------+---------------------+
   ARCHITECTURE C     |                     |                     |
   (graph)            |   cell (0, C)       |   cell (1, C)       |
                      |   reference         |   interference      |
                      |                     |   effect on C       |
                      +---------------------+---------------------+
   ARCHITECTURE D     |                     |                     |
   (prose)            |   cell (0, D)       |   cell (1, D)       |
                      |   reference         |   interference      |
                      |                     |   effect on D       |
                      +---------------------+---------------------+

   OUTCOME:  R = rho  (or FVR at fixed rho)

   main effect of surplus       : mean(1,.) - mean(0,.)
   main effect of architecture  : mean(.,C) - mean(.,D)
   INTERACTION  (the quantity of interest) :

        Delta = [ R(1,C) - R(0,C) ]  -  [ R(1,D) - R(0,D) ]

   INTERPRETATION
   +-------------------------------------------------------------------+
   | Delta > 0 : C degrades LESS under surplus than D                  |
   |             => evidence for structure under bounded verification   |
   |                even when FVR is equal (A1 fails in D's favour)     |
   |                                                                   |
   | Delta = 0 : surplus harms both equally                            |
   |             => A1 approximately true; idealised Props 2.1, 4.3     |
   |                apply; graph advantage must come from elsewhere     |
   |                                                                   |
   | Delta < 0 : C degrades MORE under surplus than D                  |
   |             => structure is a liability under clutter; H_S fails   |
   +-------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

Figure G.15. The adversarial surplus condition of §5.1. Because A1 is the load-bearing idealisation of Propositions 2.1 and 4.3, its violation must be measured as a moderator, not treated as noise. The interaction term is the only quantity that distinguishes "the graph helps" from "the graph does not help but neither does it hurt."

