G.16 Requirement Closure, Manifest Coverage and Omission

```
   Req*(c) : transitive closure of the requirement set (Def. 2.2)

                            [ c ]
                              |
              +---------------+---------------+
              |               |               |
           [ d1 ]          [ d2 ]          [ c' ]  (another claim)
              |               |               |
        +-----+-----+         |         +-----+-----+
        |           |         |         |           |
     [ e1 ]      [ e2 ]    [ e3 ]    [ d1' ]     [ e4 ]
                                        |
                                     [ e5 ]

   MANIFEST M_c AS WRITTEN          CLOSURE Req*(c) AS TRUE
   +-------------------------+      +-------------------------+
   | id(c),  h(c)            |      | id(c),  h(c)            |
   | id(d1), h(d1)           |      | id(d1), h(d1)           |
   | id(d2), h(d2)           |      | id(d2), h(d2)           |
   | id(c'), h(c')           |      | id(c'), h(c')           |
   | id(e1), h(e1)           |      | id(e1), h(e1)           |
   | id(e3), h(e3)           |      | id(e3), h(e3)           |
   | id(d1'),h(d1')          |      | id(d1'),h(d1')          |
   +-------------------------+      | id(e4), h(e4)   <-- OMITTED
                                    | id(e5), h(e5)   <-- OMITTED (2nd level)
                                    +-------------------------+

   eps_omit(c) = 1 if M_c does not cover Req*(c), else 0

   CONSEQUENCE (Cor. A.4): if e4 or e5 is removed while all listed items
   survive, the manifest check returns V on a claim with theta_b = 0.
   Under independent removal of fraction f, the FVR floor is approximately

        eps_omit * f * (1 - f)^( |M_c| )

   increasing in both eps_omit and f. This is why omission rate is a
   primary pilot outcome rather than an implementation detail.
```

Figure G.16. The closure problem in one picture. The manifest is a record of expectation; the closure is the truth about what is needed. The gap between them is the mechanism by which a trusted manifest becomes a false-verification generator. Maintaining the closure under revision, contradiction and supersession is exactly the operation that an explicit claim graph might perform better than a static manifest—and it is therefore the precise location of the graph's remaining candidate justification.